steps.reasoning_content:
The issue is that the script uses ArrowRight for navigation, but my slides use ArrowDown/ArrowRight for navigation. The script uses scroll-snap which means pressing ArrowRight may not navigate between slides properly. Let me check how the HTML navigation works.

Actually, looking at the JavaScript in my presentation:
```javascript
setupKeyboardNav() {
    document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
        if (e.key === 'ArrowDown' || e.key === 'ArrowRight' || e.key === ' ') {
            e.preventDefault();
            this.nextSlide();
        } else if (e.key === 'ArrowUp' || e.key === 'ArrowLeft') {
            e.preventDefault();
            this.prevSlide();
        }
    });
}
```

So ArrowRight should work. The issue might be that when the page loads, only the first slide is "visible" (has .visible class). The IntersectionObserver adds .visible when slides are 50% visible. But in Playwright, the initial viewport might show only part of the first slide, and the other slides aren't visible yet.

Wait, looking at the script more carefully:
1. It counts slides using selectors
2. It takes screenshot of slide 1
3. It presses ArrowRight and takes screenshots for slides 2 through slideCount

The issue is that `slideCount` is determined by DOM selectors (".slide", "[data-slide]", "section", "div > section"). In my HTML, I have `<section class="slide" ...>` which should be found.

But looking at the deduplication logic, it only keeps slides that have visual differences. The problem might be that when navigating past the last slide, the last slide is still shown (no change), so it stops early.

Actually wait, looking at the output: "Captured 3 unique slide(s)". This means the deduplication found only 3 unique slides. But I have 8 slides. 

The issue could be that scroll-snap with y mandatory means ArrowRight doesn't actually scroll to new slides—only ArrowDown would work since the slides are stacked vertically. Or the IntersectionObserver isn't triggering properly in Playwright. The deduplication stops once it encounters duplicate screenshots, so if navigation isn't advancing through all slides, it would only capture a few before hitting duplicates.

The most likely culprit is the scroll direction. Since scroll-snap-type is set to y mandatory, pressing ArrowRight won't trigger horizontal scrolling at all—it would only scroll vertically with ArrowDown. The Playwright script sends ArrowRight, but that won't move between vertically stacked slides, so the same slide stays visible and gets captured repeatedly until deduplication recognizes the duplicates and stops.

I need to either modify the script to use ArrowDown instead, or restructure my slides to scroll horizontally. Given that the user wants a 16:9 presentation and the script is already built, sending ArrowDown makes more sense than fighting against vertical scroll behavior.

The real issue is that my slides use `scroll-snap-type: y mandatory` with `scroll-behavior: smooth`, so they snap vertically. The keyboard handler intercepts ArrowRight/Left but doesn't actually scroll horizontally—it just triggers nextSlide/prevSlide. When Playwright sends ArrowRight, my handler calls `scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth' })`, but the smooth scroll might not complete within the 600ms timeout before the screenshot is taken.

Rather than trying to work around the timing, I should modify the script to use ArrowDown since the slides are vertically oriented anyway. This aligns with how the DOM is actually structured and should trigger the scroll snapping predictably.

